	spec: 'Restore the invariant that live source edits do not interrupt an opted-in web page, its draft, focus or scroll. The host still lazily builds and debounces source rebuilds, but pages requesting manual updates stay connected and receive a validated web-update event when their bundle differs, including after reconnecting to a newer host. Show one persistent, low-profile reload button directly below the tabs until the user reloads; use 16px text and a visible 1px border, an accessible name, a visible keyboard focus indicator and a target at least 24px high and 44px wide. Keep it in normal flex flow, clear of tabs and the top safe area, without replacing other notices. Clicking reload fetches the current page; draft persistence remains intact. Failed builds notify nothing; remote terminal sockets are unaffected; legacy pages retain the code-4000 compatibility behavior. Auth revocation and invalid-protocol protection retain their existing reload/error behavior. In the busy web composer, Queue precedes Steer in visual and keyboard order. A draft containing a slash-command prefix (including bare /, but not an absolute file path) displays Run instead of Send or Steer and offers no Queue button or queue hint, even when busy or showing completions. Command submission remains immediate, never queued; Alt-Enter runs slash commands normally rather than presenting a queue action. Plain-message send, steering and queue semantics, touch targets, upload handling and focus retention do not change. Behavioral checks cover manual-update notification without disconnection or page reload, stale reconnect, legacy reload and auth revocation, draft retention, action order and command-aware actions.',
